        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          "Oil-based" isn't a marketing label — it's a distinct chemistry with real consequences. Oil-based stains penetrate deeper than water-based film formers, feed dry and depleted wood instead of just coloring the surface, and weather by fading instead of peeling. They also come with real trade-offs: stronger odor, longer cure times, mineral-spirit cleanup, and outright restrictions in a handful of states. This guide is about that chemistry specifically — what it does well, where it's regulated, and which oil-based products actually earn a spot on your deck.
        </p>

        <div id="oil-chemistry" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Oil-Based Stains Actually Work: Linseed, Tung, and Alkyd Chemistry
          </h2>
          <p>
            Every deck stain either penetrates into the wood or forms a film on top of it, and that single distinction explains most of what separates oil-based products from their water-based counterparts. Oil-based stains are, almost by definition, penetrating stains — the oil molecules are small and non-polar enough to wick into the wood's capillary structure through surface tension and capillary action, carrying pigment deep into the cell walls rather than leaving it stacked on the surface.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Linseed Oil</h3>
          <p>
            Linseed oil, pressed from flax seed, is the oldest and most common carrier in traditional oil-based stains. It's a "drying oil" — meaning it doesn't dry by evaporation the way water does, it <strong>cures by oxidation</strong>, slowly reacting with oxygen in the air over days and weeks to form a flexible, cross-linked film within the wood fibers. That oxidative cure is why oil-based stains take so much longer to fully harden than water-based acrylics, and why color keeps deepening for a couple of weeks after application.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tung Oil</h3>
          <p>
            Tung oil, pressed from the seeds of the tung tree, cures faster than raw linseed oil and produces a harder, more water-resistant film once fully cured. Many premium penetrating oils — including several in this guide — blend tung oil into a linseed base specifically to speed the cure and add extra water resistance without sacrificing the deep penetration linseed provides.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Alkyd Resins</h3>
          <p>
            Alkyd resins are technically a modified oil — natural oils reacted with alcohols and acids to create a synthetic resin that retains much of oil's penetrating character while gaining more flexibility and a faster, more predictable cure. Stains built on alkyd or oil-alkyd hybrids (like Sikkens Cetol SRD, reviewed below) sit closer to the boundary between penetrating oil and thin film, trading a bit of raw penetration for a finish that resists cracking through seasonal wood movement.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Penetration vs Film: Why It Matters</h3>
          <p>
            Because oil lives inside the wood rather than on top of it, oil-based stains fail gracefully — they fade and chalk gradually as UV breaks down the oil at the surface, rather than cracking and peeling in sheets the way a surface film does when moisture gets underneath it. That's the central practical advantage of oil chemistry, and it's why refinishing an oil-stained deck is usually a clean-and-recoat job rather than a strip-and-sand ordeal.
          </p>

