        <div id="voc-restrictions" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. VOC, Odor, and Where Oil-Based Stains Are Restricted
          </h2>
          <p>
            Oil-based stains rely on petroleum solvents to keep the oil and pigment fluid enough to apply and to carry it into the wood — and those solvents are volatile organic compounds (VOCs) that off-gas as the product dries and cures. That's the direct source of the stronger smell oil-based stains are known for, and it's also the reason oil-based formulas face real regulatory limits that water-based stains generally don't.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why California Restricts Oil-Based Wood Finishes</h3>
          <p>
            California, primarily through its regional air-quality districts (most notably the South Coast Air Quality Management District) and the California Air Resources Board, enforces some of the strictest architectural-coatings VOC limits in the country. Traditional high-solvent oil-based deck stains routinely exceed those caps, which means a formula sold nationally is often unavailable in its original form in California — manufacturers either reformulate a compliant, lower-VOC version for that market, restrict distribution, or simply don't sell certain oil-based products there at all.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Other States With Similar Limits</h3>
          <p>
            California isn't alone. A number of Northeastern states that participate in Ozone Transport Commission coordination — states such as New York, New Jersey, Connecticut, and several New England states — have adopted comparable VOC limits on architectural coatings, including wood stains. Utah and a handful of other states with their own air-quality nonattainment concerns have followed with similar restrictions. The upshot: before ordering a specific oil-based stain online, especially a premium or specialty formula like TWP or Sikkens Cetol, check the manufacturer's site or the product label for state-specific availability. What ships freely to one address may be blocked or substituted with a compliant reformulation at another.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Living With the Odor</h3>
          <p>
            Even where it's fully legal, oil-based stain smells stronger and lingers longer than water-based acrylic. Plan to apply it with the area well-ventilated, keep pets and kids off the surface until the odor has substantially faded (usually 24-48 hours), and avoid working in an enclosed, covered porch or breezeway without supplementary fans. The smell fades faster outdoors in open air than in any semi-enclosed space.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-6">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-amber-700 mb-2">Before You Order an Oil-Based Stain</p>
            <ul class="space-y-2 text-amber-900 text-sm">
              <li>• Check the product label or manufacturer site for state availability, especially in CA, NY, NJ, CT, and other OTC-aligned states</li>
              <li>• Look for a "low-VOC" or "compliant" version if you're in a restricted state — several brands offer one</li>
              <li>• Plan to apply and cure in a well-ventilated outdoor space, not an enclosed porch</li>
              <li>• If odor sensitivity is a concern for your household, a water-based stain may be the more practical choice regardless of legality</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

